{
  "result": "There is a specific kind of light that only exists at 5:30 a.m. It isn't quite night and isn't quite morning. The streetlights are still on, but the sky behind them has started to turn a thin, watery blue. If you are awake then, the world feels borrowed, like you are seeing it before it has been properly set up for the day. \n\nI love that hour because everything is softer. The air is cooler and holds the memory of night. Even the harshest city sounds \u2014 the hiss of buses, the rumble of delivery trucks \u2014 are muffled, as if the city itself is still hitting the snooze button. Your thoughts are louder in that quiet, but not in an anxious way. They have room to stretch out and arrange themselves without being interrupted every three seconds by a notification or a conversation. \n\nMost people sleep through it, and I understand why. Waking up early feels like a small rebellion against comfort. But when I do catch it, with a warm mug in my hands and no real place to be yet, I feel like I have been let in on a secret. The day hasn't decided what it will be. It could be kind. It could be difficult. For those few minutes, it is simply potential, pale and open and entirely yours. \n\nAnd if you sit still long enough to watch the light change from blue to gold, you might even believe that anything is still possible today indeed.",
